  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 344 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nA warm afternoon is ongoing, with temperatures ranging from the\nupper 70s to mid 80s. Responsible for this warmth is an upper \nlevel ridge, but it will not last long. A deepening trough and \narea of low pressure to our northwest will drive a strong cold \nfront through the Northern Plains and Upper Midwest Friday. High\ntemperatures will vary considerably across the FA, ranging from\nthe 60s in the Devils Lake Basin, to near 90 in the southern \nRed River Valley, where the front will not propagate through \nuntil mid or even late afternoon. Along the front, forcing \nshould be sufficient for thunderstorm development, some of which\nmay become strong to severe. Thereafter, temperatures will \nremain seasonably cool into the weekend. Moisture will advect \nnorthward late Sunday into Monday ahead of a strong upper level \nlow to our west that will bring widespread rainfall to end the \nweekend and start the work week. Temperatures will remain \nseasonal to just a few degrees cooler than seasonal into next \nweek. \n\n...Near Critical Fire Weather this Afternoon...\n\nFor areas experiencing the most severe impacts from drought in \nsoutheastern ND, fuels have dried considerably. RH values this \nafternoon have dropped as low as 20 percent. Combine these \nfactors with a steady 10 to 20 mph southerly breeze and near \ncritical fire weather conditions are ongoing, and will continue \nthrough sunset. \n\n...Severe Storms Friday...\n\nAhead of a strong cold front, an axis of instability (2000+ \nJ/Kg of MUCAPE) will be in place over far southeastern ND into \nmuch of west central and northwestern MN. Overhead, increasing \nmidlevel west to southwest winds will improve shear profiles, \npromoting 40 knots or so of effective shear. Shear profiles will\nlikely vary across the FA. Hodographs show this well, with \nsouthern areas containing low level curvature, while northern \nareas (approximately US Highway 2 north) feature straighter \nhodographs. This will play into hazard type, with SPC \nprobabilities highlighting a 5% tornado risk extending from \nsouthern Beltrami County southward throughout the Lakes Country,\nwithin the area of better low level shear. With shear \norientation off the initiating front, supercells at least \ninitially are expected, but the strength of forcing and \nsplitting storms in the north may result in a messier cluster \nmode within a few hours of convective initiation.\n\nWhile the environment in west central MN certainly has a higher\nceiling due to the shear profiles and richer instability, there\nare questions on if storms will form. This concern is two fold.\nFirst, if the front pushes through a bit faster (some latest \nCAMs are showing this, especially within the REFS), storms may \nnot form in our area, but rather our eastern border into \nDLH/MPX's areas. Second, soundings indicate that capping will be\npresent, with capping becoming stronger the further south you \ngo within the FA. Therefore, if a combination of a faster \nfront/stronger capping comes into play, the severe risk will \nbecome more limited in west central MN, leaving more of a \nhail/wind risk in the north. \n\n...Soaking Rainfall Early Next Week...\n\nA large upper low over the Pacific Northwest will propagate \neastward towards the Northern Plains late Sunday into Monday. \nThere is some uncertainty in where the deformation band will set\nup, with NBM probabilities in the 50 to 70 percent range across\nthe entire FA. Instability while present, will not be \nsufficient for any severe weather concerns.",
